{# table; title: Plugins and tools; category: docs #}

Fey works on its own. These are the tools it uses when they are there.


  I. Completion

Inside a tag the completion knows where it is: the name, a value, a key, the value of a key. It is offered through four doors, which all give the same answers:

-  `omnifunc` (`<C-x><C-o>`), set for Fey buffers
-  {@ link, https://github.com/Saghen/blink.cmp; desc: blink.cmp @}: the source `fey`, `sources.per_filetype = { fey = { 'fey', 'buffer' } }` with the provider `fey.fey.autocompletion.blink`
-  {@ link, https://github.com/hrsh7th/nvim-cmp; desc: nvim-cmp @}: the source `fey` is registered when nvim-cmp is there: `sources = { { name = 'fey' } }`
-  `vim.lsp.enable('fey')`: the experimental language server, which also gives symbols, references and links

The trigger characters are `,` `;` `:` space `@` `#` `[` and `/`.


  II. The latex parser

The text of a `math` tag, and fenced blocks named `latex`, are highlighted with the `latex` tree-sitter parser when it is installed (nvim-treesitter installs it). Without it they are plain text.


  III. Pandoc

`pandoc` writes LaTeX, PDF, Word, OpenDocument and EPUB from the Markdown that Fey writes. Install it from your system's packages. It is the only external program the plugin calls for exports.


  IV. Icons

Checkboxes and concealed queries show icons. With a Nerd Font and `nvim-web-devicons` they are Nerd Font glyphs, otherwise plain Unicode symbols; `fey_checkbox_icons`, `fey_checkbox_icon_overrides` and `fey_conceal_icons` change them.


  V. Plugins of your own

A plugin can be configured from notes with a `plugin` tag when it is registered (`settings.plugins`, see {@ link, configuration.fey; desc: Configuration @}), and a plugin can add tags with `tag_handlers` and `tag_exports` (see {@ link, tags.fey; desc: Tags @}).
The Lua API (`require('fey.api')`) is for plugins that read the notes: the vault, the court, headings, links and the agenda; it is in `:help FeyApi`.
